\begin{quote}
Karen Jia\\
Mrs. Breakall\\
English 8 Advanced\\
27 April 2026\\
The Weight of Loving\\
A woman leaned against the foggy window, letting out a shaky breath that
she hadn't even noticed she was holding in. She could barely make out
the imposing sign of that set of sad, sullen letters that stared
unblinkingly back at her. \emph{MUNICH BANHOF}, it read. Whether it was
blurry because of the window or her tears, she couldn't tell.

The train started, and her mind flashed back.

A sobbing girl clawed desperately at her mother's woolen coat, the
already-worn texture beginning to come apart.

The mother reached down and grabbed the small, shaking wrist with such
intensity that the girl went still. In that moment, the mother's heart
shattered into a million pieces. Could her daughter feel the love that
it came from? Or would she grow to hate her for it? How she longed to
soften her grip in that moment, to smooth out the girl's messed up hair
and rub her frightened, tear-stained cheeks.

Instead, she thrust the small hand down.

\emph{It's all I can do, my darling.}

\emph{I'm sorry.}

The woman couldn't even kiss her goodbye. She couldn't even get one last
glance. She couldn't bear to.
\end{quote}

30

\begin{quote}
\textbf{A RECURRING THEME IN MY ALBUMS}\\
\textbf{All mothers must accept the inevitable:}\\
\textbf{that one day, she will have to let her child go.}\\
\textbf{But no mother ever imagines letting go like this.}

And now the woman was tormenting herself. She was swimming through an
ocean of obsidian, her heavy wool coat swallowing her whole. I know this
because I was right there, watching from the murky swamp of her guilt.

She wanted me to take her. How I, too, wished to free her from the
suffering, to take her with me right then and there. But that was
against the rules. \emph{Soon,} I wanted to assure her. \emph{Soon
you'll be free.}

And after what seemed like all of eternity, she surfaced.

The wind cut its freezing blades into her cheeks, berating her for her
negligence. She took it. Two officers in matching uniforms were ushering
the crowd off the train, dividing them between men, women, and children.
Families torn apart. Screaming. Crying. Perhaps the woman did at least
one thing right, saving the pain of separation for sooner rather than
later.

``Disinfection'' was what they were told as the breathing corpses
removed their belongings and clothing. Breathing corpses. That's what
they were, really. Walking into their own graves.
\end{quote}

31

\begin{quote}
As the book thief's mother removed the wool coat from her shoulders,
the\\
emptiness it left behind felt unnatural. Although the material was
already worn and shabby, it nevertheless offered a protection that was
now all but gone.

It was only a matter of time before the silent shrieking-- the souls of
sisters, daughters, mothers, begging me to take them. I tried working as
efficiently as I could, but you could only go so fast squeezing through
a room of packed bodies.

The book thief's mother was one of the two thousand souls that I took
away that day in that awful chamber. By the time I retrieved her, she
was glowing a faint yellow, still slightly warm as I rested her upon my
shoulder.

Later, I would find myself coming back for more and more rounds.

I truly believe that if she had to do it all over again, she would have
changed nothing. Choosing her daughter's life over her own pride, that
was love in its most heartbreaking form.

To this day, I remember the weight of the wool coat more than I remember
her face. Perhaps it was the sympathy for the mother that led me to keep
an eye out for the little book thief back on Himmel Street.
\end{quote}

32

